16.61.m:
By brahman was the earth established;
brahman established [heaven] above;
brahman established this intermediate space,
wide, upright, and crosswise.

Brahman reaches the learned Vedic scholar;
brahman this supreme one;
brahman [is] sacrifice and faith;
brahman is the mind placed in him.

Brahman created the bone;
brahman gathered the flesh;
brahman the skin and hair;
brahman brought forth the form.

Brahman created the eye;
brahman wove breath in the aperture;
brahman [created] the mind and speech;
brahman brought together the city.

16.63.m:
If after sleeping you see an evil dream,
if a wild animal runs across your path,
from sneezing, from the evil omen of a bird,
may this amulet ward [it] off!

16.67.m:
Though they are female, they call them male to me;
he who has eyes sees, the blind does not discern.
The son who is a poet, he understands this;
he who knows these shall be his father's father.

16.68.m:
With the gāyatra he measures out the hymn;
with the hymn the sāman; with the traiṣṭubha the stanza;
with the stanza, a two-footed or four-footed stanza;
with the syllable they measure out the seven voices.

With the jagatī he propped up the stream in heaven;
in the rathantara he surveyed the sun.
Three are the kindling-sticks of the gāyatra, they say;
thence by greatness it has surpassed in majesty.

I call near this cow that yields good milk;
let a milker with good hands milk her.
May Savitṛ impel for us the best impulsion;
the gharma is kindled: that I proclaim well.

This is he who whistles, by whom the cow is enveloped;
she utters her lowing, resting on the cloud.
She has brought the mortal low by her perceptions;
becoming lightning, she cast off her covering.

16.69.m:
I ask you about the farthest end of the earth;
I ask you about the navel of the world;
I ask you about the seed of the stallion;
I ask about the highest heaven of speech.

This altar is the farthest end of the earth;
this sacrifice is the navel of the world;
this soma is the seed of the stallion;
this priest is the highest heaven of speech.

16.70.m:
This for those who shout toward;
this for those who approach;
this for those who show fury.

16.71.m:
Eat with the shoots of soma;
sit on the back of Aditi.
Those men who know your wooden vessel,
O rice-mess, in the world of the path of the gods—
for them be heaven, of the measure of the vessel;
we know the earth to be your vessel.

16.72.m:
Let the Sādhyas lead this [mess] of mine from the front,
the Ādityas, Rudras, and Vasus, of one accord;
let all the gods, agreeing with the seasons,
protect this continually without negligence.

Offered in your speech, let it be offered in [your] sight;
offered in discernment, let it be offered in your strength.
In your ear, in your breath it is offered; in your offspring and immortality it is offered;
and in your desire let it be offered.

16.73.m:
Not showing wrath, come to our home,
into view, auspicious, well-disposed, not terrifying;
let that which is grown by rain come to you.

16.75.m:
From your feet, from your ankles,
from your calves, from your knees, from your thighs,
from your buttocks, around the pubic region,
from the backbone, the arṣaṇīs from the nape-tendons,
from the neck, from the shoulders,
I have destroyed the disease of your head.

16.76.m:
She is called your daughter, O Kāma, the milch-cow,
whom the poets call Speech, the Virāj;
with her avert those who are my rivals;
may Breath, offspring, cattle, life avert them!

May the watchful overseer, the swift, fierce Kāma,
make for me [a world] without rivals;
may the All-gods be my protection;
may all the gods come to this invocation of mine!

Enjoying this melted butter, rich in ghee,
may they, with Kāma as their chief, exult here;
may they make for me [a world] without rivals!

Kāma has slain those who are my rivals;
they have made broad space, prosperity for me;
let the four directions bow to me,
let the six broad spaces bring ghee to me!

16.77.m:
Not having all his heroes, he roams thrust away,
hated by friends, abandoned by his own kin;
and lightnings strike down upon the earth;
may the fierce god crush the rivals!

Kāma was born first, nothing else before [him];
neither the gods reached him, nor the ancestors, nor mortals;
therefore you are greater, ever great;
to that Kāma I make reverence!

16.78.m:
Not even the pious folk attained Kāma,
nor the gandharvas and apsarases, nor the serpents;
therefore you are greater, ever great;
to that Kāma I make reverence!

16.79.m:
Both Durṇāman and Sunāman
desire the vulva;
we strike away the Arāya;
let Sunāman desire what belongs to women!

16.81.m:
From the two-mouthed, the four-eyed,
from the five-footed, fingerless one,
from the troop creeping up,
protect all around, from the wriggler!

16.82.m:
In the leftover [remnant] are name and forms;
in the leftover is the world placed;
in the leftover are Indra and Agni;
the universe is gathered within.

In the leftover are heaven and earth,
all that has come into being is gathered;
the waters, the ocean are in the leftover;
the moon and the wind are set therein.

16.89.m:
Be born, Agni! This Aditi, in distress,
cooks a brahmaudana, desiring sons;
let the seven ṛṣis, the makers of beings,
churn you here together with offspring!

Let this great one receive the skin,
the divine earth, well-disposed;
may we reach the world of the well-doers!

16.90.m:
This is your prayer, and this your birthplace;
let Aditi, mother of heroes, receive you!
Cleanse away those who fight against this woman;
grant to him wealth consisting of all heroic sons!

16.91.m:
Let the divine ones sit down by you, the cooked oblation;
creeping out, O Agni, again him forth […]
Purified by Soma, sit in the belly of the Brahmins;
may your eaters be descendants of the ṛṣis, not [others]!

This is my immortal light, gold;
cooked from the field, this is my wish-milking cow.
This wealth I deposit among the Brahmins;
I make a path that leads to heaven among the fathers.

Be [present] for the one who toils, cooks, and presses;
make him ascend to the heavenly world,
by which he may ascend, reaching that which is the highest vigor […].

16.92.m:
Adhvaryu, wipe clean this mouth of the brown one;
make room for the clarified butter, knowing [how]!
Wipe clean all the limbs with ghee;
I make a path that leads to heaven among the fathers.

I deposit you, O brahmaudana, among the descendants of the ṛṣis;
there is no share here for non-descendants of the ṛṣis.
Agni is my guardian, and all the Maruts;
let all the gods protect what is cooked!

16.96.m:
As a man, step upon the skin of the male;
let Jātavedas not burn away your penis.
May you have a gathering of women even among the Apsarases.

16.97.m:
Slit this skin along [the flesh] with the dark [knife], O slaughterer;
joint by joint with the knife do not cut too much.
Do not harm [it]; prepare it limb by limb;
set it down firmly among the virtuous.

Place this [layer] upon the earth, you upon the earth;
pour water in, set this down.
Surround it with fire, O preparers;
let it go where the world of the virtuous is.

16.99.m:
In the eastern quarter place the head of the goat;
in the southern quarter place the right flank;
in the western quarter his rump;
in the northern quarter place the left flank.

Place the backbone of the goat in the upward quarter;
place the breast in the fixed quarter;
in the intermediate space, in the middle, his middle;
stand firmly in the quarters with four feet.

Diti and Aditi are the two horns;
truth and cosmic order are the two eyes;
the universe is his form, faith his Breath, Virāj his head.

16.100.m:
Whoever knows [him] as 'coming',
takes away the coming prosperity of his hateful rival;
he himself prospers […] […]

16.101.m:
A team of five draws its forepart;
the yoked side-horses draw along with it.
What has not moved of it is seen, not what has moved;
the farther is nearer, the nearer is farther.

Three creatures went beyond into transgression;
others settled down around the sun.
The great one stood measuring out the midspace;
he entered the green bay mares.

16.102.m:
Prajāpati moves within the womb;
unseen, he is born in many ways.
With half [of himself] he begot the whole world;
what is his [other] half, what did that beget?

Whoever knows those two fire-sticks
from which wealth is churned out,
he, knowing, would recognize the eldest;
he would know the great brāhmaṇa.

16.103.m:
You are woman, you are man;
you are girl or boy;
you, aged, totter with a staff;
you, being born, become facing every way.

He is both the eldest and the youngest;
he is both brother and father;
the one god entered into the mind,
he was born first, and he is within the womb.

Seven ṛṣis are fixed in the body;
seven guard the seat without heedlessness;
the seven waters went to the world of him who sleeps;
there are the two sleepless gods sitting at the sacrificial session.

16.108.m:
The barren cow is the mother of the noble;
the barren cow is thy mother, O Svadhā;
from the barren cow the weapon was born,
thence mind was born.

An upward drop welled up
from the hump of the Brahman;
thence thou wast born, O barren cow;
thence the hotṛ was born.

16.109.m:
From thy two fore-legs motion was born,
[...], O barren cow;
from thy entrails the [...] were born,
from thy belly the plants.

All trembled at the embryo
being born from the non-bearing one;
for she gave birth, yet they call her 'barren cow';
her relative too was fashioned by the Brahman.

Alone he unites in battles,
he who alone has power over her;
the energies became sacrifices;
the barren cow became the eye of the energetic ones.

The barren cow received the sun;
the barren cow sustained the sacrifice;
into the barren cow entered
the odana together with the Brahman.

The barren cow indeed they call immortality;
they revere the barren cow as death;
the barren cow became this all: the gods,
men, asuras, the ancestors, the ṛṣis.

Whoever might know thus, he should accept the barren cow;
for thus the sacrifice, four-footed,
yields milk to the giver, not kicking back.

16.110.m:
Some milk Soma from her;
some worship her as ghee;
they who gave the barren cow to one who knows thus,
they have gone to the third heaven of heaven.

Having given the barren cow to the Brahmins,
he attains all worlds;
for truth is placed in her,
as well as sacred lore, and heat of asceticism.

Upon the barren cow the gods live;
upon the barren cow men too;
the barren cow became this all,
as far as the sun surveys.

16.111.m:
When he addresses [them], he approaches the consecration;
when he requests water, he leads forth the waters;
those waters which are led forth at the sacrifice, those indeed are they.

The ointment which they bring, that is indeed ghee;
when they call the meal-maker, they indeed call the oblation-maker;
the rice and barley which are poured out, shoots [of Soma] are they that are strewn down.

16.112.m:
The mortar, the pestle, are the two pressing-stones;
the winnowing-basket is the strainer; the chaff is the dregs;
the ladle, the spoon, the stirring-stick, the ladle;
the water is the pressing-water; the jar is the droṇakalaśa vessel.

This indeed is the black-antelope skin, the vāyavya cups;
the host of guests makes the sacrificer a Brahmin,
when he looks at the food to be brought, [thinking,] 'May this be more!'

When he says, 'Serve out more,' thereby he makes both offspring and cattle increase;
thereby he makes his vital breath greater;
when he inquires, thereby he secures his desire;
for he asks for desire.

16.113.m:
Therefore, one should not eat [while] hating;
one should not eat the food of one who hates;
nor of one under investigation, nor of one to be investigated.

That which belongs to the guests, that is the āhavanīya fire;
that which makes the food, that is the dakṣiṇāgni fire;
that which is in the dwelling, that is the gārhapatya fire.

16.115.m:
For him the wind, the dawn sounds the hiṅ-call;
Savitṛ praises with prosperity;
Bṛhaspati sings the Udgītha with vigor; Tvaṣṭṛ responds with abundance; the All-Gods [sing] the finale.
He becomes the finale of prosperity, of offspring, and of livestock, who knows thus.

For him the rising sun sounds the hiṅ-call;
the morning forenoon praises;
noon sings the Udgītha; afternoon responds; [evening is] the finale;
[...].

For him the cloud sounds the hiṅ-call;
flashing lightning praises;
thundering, it sings the Udgītha; raining, it responds; stopping [the rain], it is the finale;
[...].

He looks at the guests: he sounds the hiṅ-call;
he addresses [them]: he praises;
he asks for water: he sings the Udgītha; he serves: he responds; the leftover [remnant] is the finale.
He becomes the finale of prosperity, of offspring, and of livestock, who knows thus.

16.116.m:
When the servers enter the dwellings,
they are indeed the cup-adhvaryus;
among them not a single one is not a Hotṛ.

16.117.m:
Invited, he partakes on the earth;
invited [in that], whatever is of all forms on the earth.
He burns on the earth, he shines on the earth;
he belongs to the heavenly world, who knows thus.

Invited, he partakes in the intermediate space;
invited [in that], whatever is of all forms in the intermediate space.
He burns in the intermediate space, he shines in the intermediate space;
[...].

Invited, he partakes in heaven;
invited [in that], whatever is of all forms in heaven.
He burns in heaven, he shines in heaven;
[...].

Invited, he partakes among the gods;
invited [in that], whatever is of all forms among the gods.
He burns among the gods, he shines among the gods;
[...].

Invited, he partakes in the worlds;
invited [in that], whatever is of all forms in the worlds.
He burns in the worlds, he shines in the worlds;
he belongs to the heavenly world, who knows thus.

16.126.m:
When, about to eat, he touches over [it] from the front,
the breath of the receiver departs forward;
rising up right from there, he wanders away;
'his head aches'—[so] he does not live beyond the twelfth [day];
and thus the overseer seizes him as he eats.

When, about to eat, he strokes around [it] from the north,
the breath of the receiver departs northward;
rising up right from there, he wanders away;
'his head aches'—[so] he does not live beyond the twelfth [day];
and thus the overseer seizes him as he eats.

When, about to eat, he touches [it] from behind facing forward,
he touches it then with shelter and with armor;
it becomes both shelter and armor for him;
he wins the porridge, he comes to possess the world of porridge, who knows thus.

16.127.m:
You are Indra's might; you are Indra's overpowering strength; you are Indra's physical strength;
you are Indra's manly power; you are Indra's bright energy; you are Indra's heroic vigor.
For victorious employment I yoke you with Indra's yoke;
for victorious employment let all forms attend upon me;
you are yoked waters.

You are Indra's might; you are Indra's overpowering strength; you are Indra's physical strength;
you are Indra's manly power; you are Indra's bright energy; you are Indra's heroic vigor.
For victorious employment I yoke you with the yoke of the waters;
for victorious employment let all forms attend upon me;
you are yoked waters.

16.130.m:
May the unstained waters [wash] the stain away from us;
fair-faced, may they carry forth away from us sin, misfortune,
forth bad dreams, forth impurity.

16.132.m:
I have gone to the heavenly light, I have gone to the light;
I have stood upon all hostile armies.
Whoever hates us, whomsoever we hate,
here I wrap up his breath and life;
here I make him go to lowest darkness.

I turn following the course of the sun, following its southward turning.
I turn around toward the luminous directions;
I turn around toward the seven ṛṣis;
I turn around toward the sacred utterance;
I turn around toward the Brahmins.
May they grant me wealth;
may they [grant] me Brahmin-splendor.

16.134.m:
[...] she went to humans.
Humans struck her;
she reconstituted herself immediately.
Therefore, every day humans bring [offerings];
they bring [offerings] to his house, who [knows thus].
